    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Japanese invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> in 1940, the communist revolutionary Hồ Chí Minh established the Việt Minh Front in 1941 to resist the occupation and win independence; cloaked in nationalism, it was designed to appeal to a wider population than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a> could command. Soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> surrendered, the Việt Minh entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a>, and on 2 September 1945 Hồ Chí Minh declared the independence of the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, replacing the Nguyễn dynasty.</p><p>The new state claimed all of Vietnam, but much of the South was not aligned with it. British troops arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 12 September 1945 and French troops took over public buildings there, while in the north the Chinese Nationalist Army came to disarm the Japanese and forced the Việt Minh to accommodate Kuomintang-supported nationalists. An election held mainly in the North in January 1946 produced a National Assembly whose party makeup was decided before the vote. The Franco-Chinese and Ho–Sainteny Agreements of March 1946 let French forces replace the Chinese north of the 16th parallel, and the Chinese troops left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiphong">Haiphong</a> in June.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Land reform was central to the state's programme. The Land Reform Law of 4 December 1953 provided for confiscating the land of landlords judged enemies of the regime, requisitioning it from others and buying it with bonds. Carried out from 1953 to 1956, it affected more than 4 million rural people, and rent reduction nearly 8 million; most land was given out without payment, with priority for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> fighters and their families. It was carried out with violence against landowners identified, sometimes incorrectly, as landlords. Estimates of those executed range up to 200,000; Gareth Porter put the figure between 800 and 2,500, Edwin E. Moise at about 5,000 and later 13,500, and Vo Nhan Tri found a party document naming 15,000 wrongful executions. Over 12,000 people died of famine in Viet Minh zones by the end of 1954.</p><p>The aim was collective farming. After a Three Year Plan (1957–1960), agricultural production reached 5.4 million tonnes, over double the levels before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> War, and by 1960 there were 40,000 cooperatives on nearly nine-tenths of all farmland. By 1961, 86 percent of farmers belonged to low-level cooperatives, and by 1971 the great majority were in high-level cooperatives without individual ownership of land.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a> (1946–54) the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> controlled most rural areas while many towns fell to the French. Chinese communist forces reached the border in 1949, and Chinese aid transformed the Viet Minh from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> militia into a standing army; with that help Võ Nguyên Giáp built a modern army that defeated the French at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Battle of Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954. Before that, in the summer of 1946, the Viet Minh colluded with French forces to eliminate Vietnamese nationalists: about 13,000 were reported destroyed in Tonkin, and another estimate gives 15,000 massacred across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><p>From 1960 the North made war on the Republic of Vietnam through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a>, sending troops and supplies down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> trail, and in 1962 it intensified the infiltration of men and materiel, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> supplied the Viet Cong with small arms and heavier weapons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the DPRK and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> gave aid and troops; against them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> supported the South. North Vietnam also sent troops to fight beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a> in the Laotian Civil War and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> in the Cambodian Civil War. After the United States withdrew by 1973, the South was swiftly overrun.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was a unitary and centralised Marxist–Leninist state with a constitution modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>'s 1936 Constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; its first constitution was adopted by the National Assembly in November 1946. Hồ Chí Minh led it from 1945 to 1960 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Lê Duẩn</a> from 1960 to 1976. In its first years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> consolidated power by terrorising and purging rival nationalist groups and Trotskyist activists, and after the Chinese withdrawal Võ Nguyên Giáp moved to secure a monopoly of power. In 1948 the state took what has been called an irreversible proletarian turn: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a>, whose membership rose from 5,000 in late 1945 to about 180,000 by the end of 1948, resolved to eradicate judicial independence and ran rectification campaigns to indoctrinate party members, soldiers and officials.</p><p>The country was divided into provinces, autonomous regions and centrally run cities. The autonomous regions, based on those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, gave tribal minorities self-government in exchange for accepting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>'s control: the Thái-Mèo Autonomous Region from 1955, the Việt Bắc Autonomous Region from 1956 and the Lào-Hà-Yên Autonomous Region from 1957. The "correction of errors" of 1956 ended the land reform, and as many as 23,748 political prisoners were released by September 1957.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The radicalisation of the state provoked large-scale defections from the maquis to areas held by the French and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a>, among them intellectuals, landlords, soldiers and cadres such as Phạm Duy and Nguyễn Văn Thiệu. After the partition of 1954 more than one million northerners went South, including an estimated 60% of the north's one million Catholics, while approximately 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> fighters and between 14,000 and 45,000 civilians moved north.</p><p>A member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a>, North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was recognised by almost all communist countries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> first, and received their aid; many non-aligned states gave it de facto recognition, and its clandestine weapon transfers during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a> made relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> close. In 1969 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> became the first Western country to extend full diplomatic recognition, by December 1972 49 countries had relations with it, and 96 did so during its existence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> agreed relations in September 1973 and, after North Vietnamese demands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, provided an endowment of 13.5 billion yen. The insurgencies it aided in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> to power in 1975.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> ended in victory rather than collapse. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and its allies withdrew from the war by 1973, and on 30 April 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> fell. For the next year the Provisional Revolutionary Government of the Republic of South Vietnam, alongside the North Vietnamese Army, governed the South, though it was seen as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> government of North Vietnam. On 2 July 1976 North and South were officially reunited as the Socialist Republic of Vietnam, whose government was dominated by holdovers from the North and which adopted the North Vietnamese constitution, flag and anthem.</p><p>The unified state kept a Soviet style of socialism as the basis of its political and economic systems and its memberships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> organisations such as COMECON until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Đổi Mới</a> economic reforms of 1986 and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1991. It went through economic decline and refugee crises and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> in 1978 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1979. The autonomous regions of the North were not continued and were fully abolished by 1978, and the collective farms were abandoned gradually in the 1980s and 1990s.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was the communist state founded by Hồ Chí Minh in 1945. It won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, ruled the North after 1954 under a constitution modelled on Stalin's, a violent land reform and collective farms, and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> brought the South under its control in 1975 before merging with it in 1976.</em></p>

